   "reference": {
    "title": "Site-specific GI neuroendocrine tumour pathology (WHO 5th)",
    "intro": "AJCC 8th edition staging and WHO 5th clinicopathological subtypes depend on the anatomical site of origin.",
    "note": "Photomicrographs are from Wikimedia Commons under open Creative Commons licences. ExpertPath pages open with your own ExpertPath login; the app holds none of their images.",
    "groups": [
     {
      "heading": "Stomach (ECL-cell NET, Types 1–3)",
      "points": [
       "Type 1 (atrophic autoimmune gastritis, hypergastrinaemia) and Type 2 (MEN1/ZES) are often multifocal, small (< 10 mm) and indolent; Type 3 is sporadic, solitary, larger (> 20 mm) and higher risk.",
       "AJCC 8th Ch. 29: pT1 <= 10 mm and lamina propria/submucosa; pT2 muscularis propria or > 10 mm."
      ],
      "links": [
       {
        "label": "ExpertPath: Gastric Neuroendocrine Tumour",
        "url": "https://app.expertpath.com/"
       }
      ],
      "images": [
       {
        "src": "assets/reference/gi_net/gastric_net.jpg",
        "caption": "Gastric well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumour: uniform organoid nests and trabeculae in the mucosa/submucosa (H&E).",
        "credit": "Mikael Häggström",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Histopathology_of_a_well-differentiated_neuroendocrine_tumor_on_a_stomach_biopsy.jpg"
       },
       {
        "src": "assets/reference/gi_net/net_chromogranin.jpg",
        "caption": "Gastric neuroendocrine tumour: diffuse strong cytoplasmic positivity for chromogranin A (chromogranin A IHC).",
        "credit": "CoRus13",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gastric_neuroendocrine_tumor_(chromogranin),_intermed._mag.jpg"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "heading": "Duodenum / Ampulla (G-cell, D-cell / somatostatinoma)",
      "points": [
       "Gastrin-producing NETs occur in the proximal duodenum; psammomatous somatostatin-producing NETs favour the periampullary region (associated with NF1).",
       "AJCC 8th Ch. 30: pT1 <= 10 mm and lamina propria/submucosa; pT2 muscularis propria or > 10 mm; pT3 pancreas/peripancreatic tissue."
      ],
      "links": [
       {
        "label": "ExpertPath: Duodenal Neuroendocrine Tumour",
        "url": "https://app.expertpath.com/"
       }
      ],
      "images": [
       {
        "src": "assets/reference/gi_net/duodenal_net.jpg",
        "caption": "Duodenal well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumour: submucosal nests and ribbons beneath Brunner glands and mucosa (H&E).",
        "credit": "Ed Uthman",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Well-differentiated_neuroendocrine_tumor_of_the_duodenum_(3047617417).jpg"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "heading": "Jejunum / Ileum (EC-cell serotonin-producing NET)",
      "points": [
       "Classic insular/nested pattern with peripheral palisading, eosinophilic granules, mesenteric fibrosis and elastic vascular sclerosis; CDX2+.",
       "AJCC 8th Ch. 31: pN2 = >= 12 regional nodes OR large mesenteric mass(es) > 20 mm."
      ],
      "links": [
       {
        "label": "ExpertPath: Small Intestinal Neuroendocrine Tumour",
        "url": "https://app.expertpath.com/"
       }
      ],
      "images": [
       {
        "src": "assets/reference/gi_net/ileal_net.jpg",
        "caption": "Small intestinal (ileal) neuroendocrine tumour: solid insular nests with peripheral palisading and desmoplastic stroma (H&E).",
        "credit": "Nephron",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Small_intestine_neuroendocrine_tumour_intermed_mag.jpg"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "heading": "Appendix (EC-cell and L-cell NET)",
      "points": [
       "Usually incidental at the appendiceal tip; size (> 20 mm) and mesoappendiceal invasion are key for right hemicolectomy decisions.",
       "Goblet cell adenocarcinoma is NO LONGER classified as a NET in WHO 5th edition (report under Appendix Carcinoma)."
      ],
      "links": [
       {
        "label": "ExpertPath: Appendiceal Neuroendocrine Tumour",
        "url": "https://app.expertpath.com/"
       }
      ],
      "images": [
       {
        "src": "assets/reference/gi_net/appendiceal_net.jpg",
        "caption": "Appendiceal well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumour at the tip of the appendix (H&E).",
        "credit": "Adel Elkbuli et al.",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Histopathology_of_appendiceal_carcinoid.jpg"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "heading": "Colon / Rectum (L-cell and EC-cell NET)",
      "points": [
       "Rectal L-cell NETs show trabecular/ribbon architecture, express SATB2 and PAP, and may be chromogranin-negative (synaptophysin/INSM1+).",
       "AJCC 8th Ch. 33: pT1a < 10 mm; pT1b 10–20 mm; pT2 muscularis propria or > 20 mm."
      ],
      "links": [
       {
        "label": "ExpertPath: Colorectal Neuroendocrine Tumour",
        "url": "https://app.expertpath.com/"
       }
      ]
     }
    ]
   }
